cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you refer to this pre-registration, please cite it as below. It contains no results yet; cite a later dated commit for those."
title: "harness-abc-bench: a pre-registered A/B/C benchmark of coding-harness frameworks on brownfield work"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Dziatkovskii
    given-names: Anton
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7408-3054"
    affiliation: "Palo Alto AI Research Lab"
repository-code: "https://github.com/tonydzi/harness-abc-bench"
url: "https://github.com/tonydzi/harness-abc-bench"
date-released: 2026-08-11
abstract: >-
  A pre-registration, frozen 2026-08-11 before any run: do coding-harness frameworks beat a
  disciplined baseline on real brownfield maintenance work? Three arms - plain Claude Code
  plus house rules, Superpowers v6.2.0 with its review skills disabled, and OpenSpec v1.8.0
  as a spec layer only - run three maintenance tasks each in a latin square over a live
  operations codebase of about 2650 Python files, with Spec Kit held as a reserve arm on
  named triggers. Metrics, hidden characterization-test oracles written outside the arm
  worktrees, and the adopt and auto-reject thresholds are all published in advance, with the
  7-day keep rate as the tie-breaker, because every comparable evaluation the authors found
  ran on greenfield demos and reported its criteria afterwards. Results, and the decision,
  are committed to be published here whichever arm wins, including the baseline. This
  record documents the design only; no measurement exists in it.
keywords:
  - pre-registration
  - benchmark
  - ai-coding-agents
  - claude-code
  - superpowers
  - openspec
  - brownfield
  - software-maintenance
